# AdGuard Home Fork Change Log

## AdGuard now keeps a rotating internet address published to Namecheap

The house has a dynamic public IP and a Namecheap domain, and nothing was
keeping the two in agreement. AdGuard Home is the obvious place to put that: it
is already the DNS perimeter, it already runs continuously on the LAN, and it
already resolves through configured upstreams — so the updater inherits a
process whose whole job is being right about DNS.

New package internal/amberddns/, wired in from internal/home/amberddns.go. The
fork adds no fields to upstream types except one status field, and the whole
surface is four upstream lines: a /control/status block, the bus registration
(in the fork's own file), the start call, and a SIGHUP hook.

NOT THE NAMECHEAP API. api.namecheap.com authenticates with an API key and
requires the caller's address to be whitelisted in advance, which cannot work
when the address changing is the entire problem; its setHosts method also
replaces a domain's whole record set, so moving one A record with it risks
deleting every other record on the domain. The dynamic DNS endpoint updates one
record, authenticates with the per-domain dynamic DNS password, and has neither
problem.

THREE TRAPS, ALL CONFIRMED AGAINST THE LIVE ENDPOINT rather than assumed. A
rejected update answers HTTP 200. A rejected update answers <Done>true</Done>.
And the XML declares encoding="utf-16" over an ASCII body, served as
Content-Type: application/json — which makes Go's xml.Decoder refuse the
document outright until given a CharsetReader that passes the bytes through
unchanged, correct precisely BECAUSE the declaration is wrong. ErrCount is the
only field that means it worked. Each has a regression test built on the
captured response.

TWO BUGS THE TESTS FOUND, both of which would have been silent in production.
An HTML error page from a proxy or captive portal decoded into a zero-valued
struct whose ErrCount of 0 read as a successful publish; the root element is
now pinned, so silence cannot read as success. And the password is a query
parameter, so *url.Error printed the full URL — a redacted format string with a
raw error wrapped into it leaked the credential into the log anyway. The
message is now scrubbed while the error chain stays intact for errors.Is.

WHAT IT REFUSES TO DO. It will not publish an RFC 1918, CGNAT, loopback,
link-local, reserved or IPv6 address — a reflector behind a proxy returns a
perfectly valid address that is simply not ours, and under CGNAT there is no
public address to publish at all, which it names so the operator takes it to
the ISP rather than to this config. It will not act on a change reported by one
reflector: an apparent move is confirmed against a different service first,
because that is the only moment a wrong answer repoints the domain at a
stranger, and in steady state it costs nothing. It will not hammer a provider
that has rejected it — a wrong password is a configuration fault and will still
be wrong in five minutes, so rejections back off on a slower schedule than
network failures.

Publication state is per record, not per domain, which is what makes a partial
failure recover: the host that succeeded is left alone and the one that failed
is retried, where a shared marker would either re-send everything or mark the
domain current and never retry the failure at all. And an unchanged address is
still re-asserted every force_refresh_interval, because the manager's idea of
what is published is a belief that a hand edit or a restart can falsify without
it noticing.

A bad config logs loudly and does NOT stop AdGuard Home. This process is the
network's resolver; refusing DNS to the whole house over a wrong dynamic DNS
password would be far worse than the fault being reported. It is visible on
/control/status under dynamic_dns and over the bus as adguard.ddns.status, both
read-only — triggering an update from the bus would be a write function and
still needs its own contract change.

Config is a separate fork-owned file, amber_ddns.yaml, not AdGuardHome.yaml:
upstream owns that schema and migrates it, and it is no place for a live
credential. The password comes from an environment variable by preference, and
a literal one is refused unless the file is mode 600.

Verified: 46 tests where there were none; go test ./... green across the repo;
go vet clean; race detector clean; 91.9% statement coverage on the new package.
| Operator Agent

## swept: Connector: stop answering the wrong question, stop reaching into globalContext

THE WRONG ANSWER. A caller could ask the query log for one client's lookups
and get the whole log back with ok:true. The handler appended search, domain
and client to one url.Values key; AdGuard reads it with Get, which returns
only the first, so two filters were accepted and silently discarded.
querylog.Search returns `any`, so the response cannot be filtered afterwards —
the fix is at request level: any one of the three is accepted, more than one
is refused naming the offenders. Also caps the limit, which the bus manifest
promised and nothing enforced, and reports the applied limit back.

THE DRAWER. The three globalContext reaches are replaced by narrow interfaces
resolved in ONE function that takes what it is given and falls back to the
global for anything absent — so both forms work while upstream dismantles it,
and when a field goes the compiler stops in one place, loudly. stats.Interface
and querylog.QueryLog are already interfaces upstream, so those two will need
no change at all when they are handed in; only clientsContainer needed a
shape. Handlers moved onto their own type, so the fork adds no fields to
upstream structs.

Also fixes a suppressed signal: a stopped AdGuard hid protection_disabled
because one guard covered both severity and the signal list. Severity is now
derived from the signals. recommended_actions, empty since day one, says
something.

Verified: 9 tests where there were none, including regression tests for both
bugs; go test ./... green; RCP-CLEANUP-BUILD-d3bb32b860d022d6 exit [0,0,0].

